, "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n,\n    such that each element is a positive integer, greater than or equal to\n    twice the previous element, and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    _memo = {}  # Memoization dictionary: maps (current_length, last_element) to count.\n\n    def _count_sequences_recursive(current_length: int, last_element: int) -> int:\n        # Base case: A sequence of length n has been formed.\n        if current_length == n:\n            return 1\n\n        # Check memoization cache.\n        if (current_length, last_element) in _memo:\n            return _memo[(current_length, last_element)]\n\n        total_sequences_from_here = 0\n        min_next_element = last_element * 2\n\n        # If the minimum possible next element exceeds m, no further sequence can be formed.\n        if min_next_element > m:\n            _memo[(current_length, last_element)] = 0\n            return 0\n\n        # Recursively explore all valid next elements.\n        for next_element in range(min_next_element, m + 1):\n            total_sequences_from_here += _count_sequences_recursive(current_length + 1, next_element)\n\n        # Store the result in the memoization cache.\n        _memo[(current_length, last_element)] = total_sequences_from_here\n        return total_sequences_from_here\n\n    total_count = 0\n    # Determine the maximum possible value for the first element to form a sequence of length n.\n    # The smallest possible last element for a sequence of length n, starting with x, is x * (2^(n-1)).\n    # So, x * (2^(n-1)) <= m => x <= m / (2^(n-1)).\n    max_first_element = m // (2**(n - 1)) if n > 0 else 0\n\n    # Iterate through all possible starting elements for the sequence.\n    for first_element in range(1, max_first_element + 1):\n        total_count += _count_sequences_recursive(1, first_element)\n\n    return total_count", 